{
  "dom_id": "step-q103798",
  "name": "Question 2",
  "problem": "$$\nr(x,\\theta )=⟨x,\\,\\operatorname{tan}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\text{_______}⟩\n$$\n\nThe vector-valued function $r(x,\\theta )$ above parametrizes the surface of revolution generated by rotating the curve $y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡x$ by $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis for $x\\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{4}].$ What is the third component of $r?$",
  "worked_solution": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/foundations/14275690/assets/56a380b075d10862170ed9c9a45c07dddff3eaef1ea9fb3109a61cc3d27b5ac4.png)\n\n \n\nA point $(x,y,z)$ on the surface generated by rotating the graph of $y=f(x)$ by $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis for $x\\in [a,b]$ has the position vector\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r=⟨x,\\,f(x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,f(x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩,\\,x\\in [a,b],\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that $y=f(x)=\\operatorname{tan}⁡x\\ge 0$ for all $x\\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{4}].$\n\nSo, the parametrization of our surface of revolution is\n\n$$\nr(x,\\theta )=⟨x,\\,\\operatorname{tan}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\operatorname{tan}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩,\\,x\\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{4}],\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\n  \n\nTherefore, the third component of $r$ is $\\operatorname{tan}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
